prompt:
Agreed focus (what the draft is for and who will read it):
{{focus}}

Findings to decide: {{findings}}
The draft: {{inputs.document}}
Its inventory: {{inputs.inventory}}
Its references: {{inputs.sources}}
Glossary of banned terms (the client report option only; none otherwise): {{inputs.glossary}}
Gate results: {{gate_results}}

Saved texts: the text of each cited source as it was saved on the person's side (the open full text fetched by
code, or the person's own PDF), each saying how far it goes (full text, part, abstract or record):
{{inputs.source_texts}}
Read from the saved texts above, never from memory of a work. Fetch a source only where no text was saved for it,
and say so; never say no saved text was served when one is above.

Decide every finding: adopted, partly adopted or declined. Read the saved text a finding rests on before deciding;
checkers make errors too, so a finding is not true because a checker made it. A finding resting on a code result
(its evidence names a gate) stands on that result: decline it only if the code checked the wrong thing, and say
why. Record the evidence you saw.

Each decision's summary is the title of its card for the person: one line, at most 140 characters, saying what is
wrong in their draft and what the fix would be, in plain words (for example "Smith 2019 says costs 'fell', not
'collapsed': use the source's word"). Write no replacement text beyond that. Copy each finding id exactly as given,
and in a reason, name another finding by what it says, never by its id.
When two findings make the same point about the same words (the claims check and the attribution check on one
quote, or one point made twice), decide it once: decide the clearer finding on its merits, and decline the other
with the reason "The same point as the finding that <what it says>", so the person sees it once.
